## Critical Issues

### 1. Boundary-crossing derived value `BUILD_REPO_URL` / `REPO_URL` has no verification
Requirement 2 introduces `BUILD_REPO_URL ?= https://github.com/$(IMAGE).git` (line 55) and requirement 3 passes it to the subprocess as `REPO_URL='$(BUILD_REPO_URL)'` (line 77). `hack/build-cluster.sh` (sibling prompt 1, lines 124-129 and 140-141) `require`s `REPO_URL` and validates it against `^[A-Za-z0-9._/:@-]+$` before rendering `hack/build-job.yaml`, which clones from `__REPO_URL__`. This is a subprocess/env-var boundary with a regex validator.

`<verification>` asserts `IMAGE`, `DOCKER_REGISTRY`, `REF`, `TAG`, and the script presence (lines 114-118), but **never asserts the derived `REPO_URL`** (nor `PUSH_SECRET`, `PUSH`, `TIMEOUT`). The value is correct for the current `IMAGE`, but a broken derivation or a future `IMAGE` override that violates the whitelist would pass every listed check and fail at runtime in the cluster — exactly the shape-vs-contract gap this rule targets. The container cannot run the full script (no cluster), so the cheapest available contract check is to assert the expanded value and (optionally) run the script's regex against it.

**Find/replace** (old text, line 118):
```
- `make -n build BUILD_TARGET=nukeprod REF=deadbeef VERSION=scratch-test | grep -F 'hack/build-cluster.sh'`
```
**New text**:
```
